-{"id": "RELEASE-LORE-007", "title": "[P3] releng lane workdir is not a git work tree — every lore release tick must hunt for the real checkout before it can gather evidence", "status": "pending", "priority": "P3", "created_by": "releng-lane lore-releng-2026-11-16-23-09-49", "reasoning": "The lane workdir is /home/kara/.hermes/stand-in/releng-lane/lore. It contains .coding-hermes/board/ but `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` exits 128 there, so it is not a work tree. A release tick needs git history, tags and remote refs, so this tick had to locate and verify /home/kara/get-h3/lore before it could gather a single piece of evidence, and could not tell from the lane path whether the stand-in board mirrored the repo board (it did, byte-identical, but only after a comparison). Either point the lane at the git checkout or make the stand-in a real worktree; a board-only stand-in silently makes the project un-releasable from the path the scheduler hands the lane.", "detail": "Repro: cd /home/kara/.hermes/stand-in/releng-lane/lore && git rev-parse --show-toplevel -> exit 128. Real checkout: /home/kara/get-h3/lore (remote get-h3/lore, branch main). Note the same class of hazard the QA identity incident covered: the lane workdir lives in more than one store (projects.workdir column, fleet.toml, and a per-row prompt \"Workdir:\" line) and the stand-in can disagree with the repo.", "source": "releng-lane sweep 2026-11-16", "acceptance_criteria": ["From the lane workdir named by the scheduler, `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` exits 0 and resolves to a checkout whose `git remote -v` names get-h3/lore.", "The lane workdir resolves to the same git object store used for the next release cut, or the lane prompt names the real checkout explicitly so no tick has to search for it.", "A releng tick can run `scripts/release.sh --dry-run` from the path it is given without cd-ing anywhere else."], "capability_tags": ["releng", "lane-config", "infra"], "complexity": "low", "created_at": "2026-11